KMSDRM: submit the frame to the GPU before waiting for the previous page flip.

KMSDRM_GLES_SwapWindow() waited for the pending page flip first and only then
called eglSwapBuffers(). On a tiled GPU the swap's flush is where the frame's
render job is submitted, so the GPU sat idle from the end of the previous
frame's job until the previous flip completed, and each frame's render could
start only at a vblank. Any frame whose GPU time plus the display's latch
margin exceeded one vblank interval was then shown on every second vblank:
exactly 30.00 fps at 60 Hz, although the CPU and the GPU could each keep up
with 60 (docs/gpu-new-lane/frame-pacing.md: quake2-drm, 8.8 ms CPU + 14.8 ms
GPU per frame, 30.00 fps in every window).

Swap first, lock the new front buffer, then wait for the pending flip, release
the old front buffer and queue the new flip. At most one flip is still pending
at a time and no buffer is released before the flip that replaces it on screen
has completed, so there is no tearing; the surface needs three buffers (on
screen, queued, being rendered), which GBM provides. SDL_VIDEO_DOUBLE_BUFFER=1
keeps its extra wait right after the flip. Not Phoenix-specific.

